---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import ImageTool from '../components/ImageTool.tsx';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import { webApplicationLd, proseClass } from './_seo';
const title = 'Convert WebP to JPG Online, No Upload | SizeTune';
const description =
  'Turn a WebP image into a JPG in your browser, with no upload and no sign-up. Learn why sites give you WebP, what changes, and what to do if a form refuses it.';
const jsonLd = webApplicationLd('SizeTune: convert WebP to JPG', '/webp-to-jpg/', description);
---
<Base pageType="tool" width="wide" title={title} description={description} path="/webp-to-jpg/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Hero title="Convert WebP to JPG" lead="Add a WebP picture and download it as a JPG. The tool opens with JPEG chosen and no size limit. If a form also states a limit in KB, set it in the tool." />

  <ImageTool client:load defaultFormat="jpeg" defaultNoLimit label="WebP to JPG converter" />
  <noscript>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 p-4">
      This converter works inside your browser, so it will not run while JavaScript is turned off. Enable JavaScript and
      reload to convert your WebP file. The notes below on why WebP files appear still apply with any other tool.
    </p>
  </noscript>
  <p class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-3xl text-center text-base text-slate-700">
    Need the JPG under a size limit for a form? <a data-testid="to-resizer" class="font-medium text-teal-800 underline underline-offset-4" href="/">Open the KB resizer</a>.
  </p>

  <div class={`mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-10 ${proseClass}`}>
    <h2>Why you end up with a WebP file</h2>
    <p>
      WebP is an image format made to keep pictures small on the web. Many websites now serve it automatically, so when
      you right-click a picture and choose "save image as", you can get a .webp file even though the page looked like
      it showed an ordinary photo. Some chat apps and screenshot tools also produce it. The format is perfectly good, but
      plenty of upload forms, older office programs and print services were built around JPG and PNG and refuse
      anything else.
    </p>

    <h2>What changes when WebP becomes JPG</h2>
    <p>
      A WebP picture can be saved in two ways, with some detail thrown away to save space or with every pixel kept. A JPG
      always throws some detail away. Converting a lossy WebP to a JPG therefore compresses the picture a second time, and
      the new file may show faint blockiness around sharp edges that was not there before. The converter saves at quality 85
      out of 100, a common good-quality setting, to keep that change small, and the preview lets you compare before you download.
    </p>
    <p>
      JPG cannot store transparency. If your WebP has see-through areas, such as a cut-out logo, those areas are filled
      with a colour you choose. White is the default, and black or a custom colour are also offered. If the picture
      must stay transparent, convert it to PNG instead on the <a href="/">main tool</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The file size can go either way. A photo often grows a little, because WebP is usually more compact than JPG at a
      similar look. A flat graphic can grow a lot more.
    </p>

    <h2>Steps</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Add the .webp file by dragging it onto the box or choosing it.</li>
      <li>Leave the output as JPEG. Choose a background colour if the picture has transparent parts.</li>
      <li>Look at the preview and download the result when it looks right.</li>
      <li>If your form states a limit, type it as the maximum size and the tool converts again to fit.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>If the form still rejects the file</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Check the name ends in .jpg or .jpeg. The tool names the download with the right ending.</li>
      <li>Read the limit again. A form that wants 100 KB or less will refuse a 400 KB file whatever its format.</li>
      <li>Check the pixel size the form asks for. Format and size are separate rules.</li>
      <li>See <a href="/check-image-size/">the image checker</a> to compare the file with your form's rules.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about WebP to JPG</h2>
    <h3>Is converting WebP to JPG lossless?</h3>
    <p>No. JPG always discards some detail, and a WebP that was already compressed loses a little more. At the high quality used here the change is small.</p>
    <h3>Why is my JPG bigger than the WebP?</h3>
    <p>WebP usually stores the same picture in fewer bytes. A larger JPG is normal, and you can set a size limit in the tool if a form needs it smaller.</p>
    <h3>Can I convert an animated WebP?</h3>
    <p>The tool makes a single still picture from the first frame. It does not keep animation.</p>
    <h3>Does the picture get uploaded?</h3>
    <p>No. The conversion runs in your browser. Read the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for details.</p>
    <h3>Can I convert PNG files too?</h3>
    <p>Yes. Use the <a href="/png-to-jpg/">PNG to JPG page</a>.</p>
  </div>
  <RelatedTools path="/webp-to-jpg/" />
</Base>
